/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?fit=5908%2C3081&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"5908,3081\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"A_1 Yanling img2 (matching img1)\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?fit=1024%2C534&amp;ssl=1\" data-id=\"846\" src=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?resize=5908%2C3081&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-846\" srcset=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?w=5908&amp;ssl=1 5908w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?resize=300%2C156&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?resize=1024%2C534&amp;ssl=1 1024w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?resize=768%2C401&amp;ssl=1 768w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?resize=1536%2C801&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?resize=2048%2C1068&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?resize=1200%2C626&amp;ssl=1 1200w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A_1-Yanling-img2-matching-img1-edited.png?w=3000&amp;ssl=1 3000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px\" \/><\/figure>\n<\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>A.3 Warp the Images<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Nearest Neighbor Interpolation<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In this method, each pixel in the warped image is assigned the value of the nearest pixel from the source image.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Step 1: Calculate image boundaries and mesh grid<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">I first project the four image corners through <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=H&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"H\" class=\"latex\" \/> to find the output image\u2019s bounding box <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=x_%7B%5Cmin%7D%2C+x_%7B%5Cmax%7D%2C+y_%7B%5Cmin%7D%2C+y_%7B%5Cmax%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"x_{&#92;min}, x_{&#92;max}, y_{&#92;min}, y_{&#92;max}\" class=\"latex\" \/>. Then, a mesh grid of all integer coordinates (using <code><mark style=\"background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);color:#cf2e2e\" class=\"has-inline-color\">np.meshgrid<\/mark><\/code>) in this range is created to represent each target pixel location.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Step 2: Inverse Mapping<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The homography <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=H&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"H\" class=\"latex\" \/> defines a transformation <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=p%27+%3D+H+p&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"p&#039; = H p\" class=\"latex\" \/>. Therefore, I use the inverse homography <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=H%5E%7B-1%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"H^{-1}\" class=\"latex\" \/> to map each target pixel <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=%28x%27%2C+y%27%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"(x&#039;, y&#039;)\" class=\"latex\" \/> back to its corresponding source coordinate <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=%28x%2C+y%29&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"(x, y)\" class=\"latex\" \/>.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">$$<br>p = H^{-1} p&#8217;<br>$$<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Step 3 (Nearest neighbor): Rounding coordinates (nearest neighbor)<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For each projected point, I normalize by the homogeneous coordinate and round the result to the nearest integer pixel location:<br>$$<br>x = \\text{round}\\left(\\frac{x&#8217;}{w&#8217;}\\right), \\quad<br>y = \\text{round}\\left(\\frac{y&#8217;}{w&#8217;}\\right)<br>$$<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Step 4: Create a mask to decide which coordinates are within the range<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When performing inverse warping, some of the mapped coordinates <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=%28x%2C+y%29+&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\"(x, y) \" class=\"latex\" \/> may fall outside the valid range of the original image (e.g., negative indices or values greater than the image width\/height). To address this, I create a binary mask <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=+%5Calpha+&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\" &#92;alpha \" class=\"latex\" \/> that marks which pixels in the warped image correspond to valid source coordinates.<br>Each pixel satisfies the following condition:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">$$<br>\\alpha = (x \\ge 0) \\land (x &lt; w_{\\text{original}}) \\land (y \\ge 0) \\land (y &lt; h_{\\text{original}})<br>$$<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Step 5: Assign the pixels<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">I first initialized a canvas with all 0 values (black), and then assigned the pixels back to their locations according to the mask:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">$$<br>I'(x&#8217;, y&#8217;) =<br>\\begin{cases}<br>I(x, y), &amp; \\text{if } \\alpha(x&#8217;, y&#8217;) = 1 \\\\<br>0, &amp; \\text{otherwise}<br>\\end{cases}<br>$$<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Bilinear Interpolation<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The process largely follows the same inverse mapping structure as the nearest neighbor method, but with continuous interpolation steps instead of rounding:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Step 3 (Bilinear): Finding all four neighbors<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In this step, instead of rounding the coordinates, I found all four neighbors by computing <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=+x_%7B%5Ctext%7Bfloor%7D%7D%2C+x_%7B%5Ctext%7Bceil%7D%7D%2C++y_%7B%5Ctext%7Bfloor%7D%7D%2C+y_%7B%5Ctext%7Bceil%7D%7D+&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\" x_{&#92;text{floor}}, x_{&#92;text{ceil}},  y_{&#92;text{floor}}, y_{&#92;text{ceil}} \" class=\"latex\" \/> to get all the neighboring pixel values <img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/s0.wp.com\/latex.php?latex=+I_%7B11%7D%2C+I_%7B12%7D%2C+I_%7B21%7D+%2CI_%7B22%7D&#038;bg=ffffff&#038;fg=000&#038;s=0&#038;c=20201002\" alt=\" I_{11}, I_{12}, I_{21} ,I_{22}\" class=\"latex\" \/>.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The weights are determined by the pixel\u2019s distance to each neighbor, and the four contributions are summed to produce the interpolated color value:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">$$<br>I(x, y) =<br>(x_{\\text{ceil}} &#8211; x)(y_{\\text{ceil}} &#8211; y) I_{11} +<br>(x_{\\text{ceil}} &#8211; x)(y &#8211; y_{\\text{floor}}) I_{12} +<br>(x &#8211; x_{\\text{floor}})(y_{\\text{ceil}} &#8211; y) I_{21} +<br>(x &#8211; x_{\\text{floor}})(y &#8211; y_{\\text{floor}}) I_{22}<br>$$<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This produces smoother results compared to nearest neighbor interpolation, though at a higher computational cost.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Below are several examples performing the two warping methods. As the image size is quite small (600 by 375 pixels), the differences between the two results is not obvious.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery has-nested-images columns-1 is-cropped wp-block-gallery-5 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"241\" data-attachment-id=\"874\" data-permalink=\"https:\/\/shaoyiwang.com\/?attachment_id=874\" data-orig-file=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/shaoyiwang.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2025\/10\/A3_Tianlu_1.png?fit=1415%2C333&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1415,333\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}\" data-i